Date: Wed, 3 Sep 2025 12:08:39 +0000
Subject: Revert "mm/hugetlb: fix huge_pmd_unshare() vs GUP-fast race"

This reverts commit 034a52b5ef57c9c8225d94e9067f3390bb33922f.

The original commit, which fixes CVE-2025-38085 (CVSS: 5.5 Medium), introduced a
significant performance regression in shared memory operations. Performance
testing shows a 4x slowdown in affected workloads.

The regression occurs because huge_pmd_unshare() calls
tlb_remove_table_sync_one() which broadcasts IPIs to all cores to ensure proper
TLB synchronization, leading to a significant latency increase for machines with
a big amount of CPUs.

This regression was discovered after the patch was ingested through a stable
rebase. We reported this to upstream memory management maintainers to develop an
alternative fix that doesn't impact performance:
https://lore.kernel.org/all/4d3878531c76479d9f8ca9789dc6485d@amazon.de

Given the medium severity of the CVE (5.5), we're temporarily reverting this
patch while a proper fix is developed.

Signed-off-by: Simon Liebold <simonlie@amazon.de>

diff --git a/mm/hugetlb.c b/mm/hugetlb.c
--- a/mm/hugetlb.c
+++ b/mm/hugetlb.c
@@ -7274,13 +7274,6 @@ int huge_pmd_unshare(struct mm_struct *mm, struct vm_area_struct *vma,
 		return 0;
 
 	pud_clear(pud);
-	/*
-	 * Once our caller drops the rmap lock, some other process might be
-	 * using this page table as a normal, non-hugetlb page table.
-	 * Wait for pending gup_fast() in other threads to finish before letting
-	 * that happen.
-	 */
-	tlb_remove_table_sync_one();
 	ptdesc_pmd_pts_dec(virt_to_ptdesc(ptep));
 	mm_dec_nr_pmds(mm);
 	return 1;
